SM(170,{"n":"James 2.14-26","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/James 2.14-26.mp3","d":3074.9,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[9.04,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.64,"So we'll go ahead and get started."],[22.64,"When we get started here in just a moment, we'll pick up in the Epistle of St."],[28.4,"James, chapter 2, verse 14."],[31.36,"As I was just saying, a completely non controversial passage in any way that no one has ever had an argument about."],[41.28,"And now I will say, and I think we're going to see tonight that this should be a passage about which there's not a lot of controversy."],[53.56,"But what causes controversy is not that there's something shocking or weird or bizarre here in the middle of James chapter 2."],[62.28,"It's that since at least Martin Luther, there's been a certain reading of St."],[70.68,"Paul's Epistles, as we talked about in the introduction to St."],[74.44,"James. And the sort of plain meaning of what we're going to see in the second half of chapter 2 of St."],[83.0,"James's Epistle, the plain reading of it directly contradicts that reading of St."],[89.32,"Paul. And so this then creates a dilemma for people who have been convinced of that reading."],[99.16,"One possible answer would be to just say, well, perhaps we've read St"],[105.76,"Paul incorrectly. That has been the minority approach taken, including by Martin Luther himself."],[116.32,"And the much more common approach, including that taken essentially by Martin Luther himself, although there's some ambiguity, if you read his his Martin Luther's actual commentary on the Epistle of St."],[130.16,"James, it's not as clear as I'm about to make it."],[132.4,"So this is an oversimplification."],[134.32,"Don't at me, Lutherans, uh, but uh is to just take the approach that uh St."],[141.92,"James and St."],[142.72,"Paul are opposed to each other."],[145.36,"And this, as you move forward in particularly German scholarship, past Martin Luther once you get to the point where in the uh 18th century and onward, where uh folks are no longer folks no longer feel beholden to the idea that what's in the Bible is true, uh, then it becomes very easy to just say, well, yeah, they disagree."],[171.8,"And so then once you start down the road of what St."],[176.12,"James says here in this one part of this one chapter, he's saying the opposite of what St."],[181.96,"Paul is saying, uh, in particularly Galatians and Romans, uh, then that became the basis as you go through the 18th and 19th centuries of constructing this whole edifice of two different Christianities in the early church."],[203.12,"That on one hand you have this sort of Pauline Christianity that's mainly Gentiles, and that gets sort of more and more caricatured."],[212.82,"Until it's the point where St."],[214.1,"Paul is like anti-Jewish and hates the law, hates the Torah, is going out telling everybody to stop keeping kosher, and that's like his whole mission in life."],[223.03,"Uh, almost to the point that where they're trying to make him a Gnostic or something."],[227.03,"And then on the other side, Saint James becomes uh this person who's not even really a Christian, if you go far enough."],[236.92,"He's just this Jewish leader who thinks his brother was a good rabbi and doesn't believe that Christ is divine in any sense and is leading this kind of Jewish Christianity and that the two are opposed to each other."],[251.28,"And St. James' Epistle just kind of shoehorned itself into the New Testament by virtue of him being prominent."],[260.4,"So they sort of couldn't leave it out even if they wanted to, or something."],[265.73,"Now, obviously, that narrative makes about zero historical sense once you get to like the formation of the New Testament canon."],[277.35,"That where you find that there are all of these communities all over the world in the second and third centuries that are mostly Gentiles right mostly non-Jewish Christians and are using both St."],[293.71,"Paul's epistles and the Epistle of St."],[295.71,"James authoritatively. And not only that, but don't seem to think there's a conflict."],[303.45,"So part of the presupposition here is that somehow St."],[307.93,"Paul and St."],[308.73,"James were very clearly directly contradicting each other, but nobody noticed it until the 16th century."],[315.53,"And when I say nobody noticed it, I mean nobody noticed it."],[319.79,"You could you could go through, there's a recent book where um it's actually a published dissertation, um, where he went he went through literally everything that talks about the works of the law in St."],[333.99,"Paul, in the Church Fathers, until the year 300."],[338.87,"Literally everything we have that mentions it or alludes to it."],[343.91,"And not one of those early sources understands St."],[349.75,"Paul the way Luther did."],[352.75,"Not one. So the additional presupposition, then, is that uh those who lived closest in time to St."],[365.71,"Paul, in the churches St."],[367.71,"Paul helped found. who are from the Gentiles, who everyone agrees are St"],[372.63,"Paul's people, all completely misunderstood the plain meaning of what he wrote, and then Martin Luther figured it out in Germany in the 16th century."],[387.16,"You literally have to get to that point."],[389.4,"And to their credit, I have known reformed people in particular, who will literally talk about church history that way."],[398.36,"There's the Pauline gospel, it gets lost by the end of the first century, and it gets recovered by Martin Luther in the 16th century."],[407.84,"And I give credit to them for being consistent."],[413.5,"Because that is the position you're forced to by all of the historical evidence if you're gonna say that Luther was reading it right."],[425.34,"You're forced to that."],[428.78,"So there's another option, though, for people who have some sympathy for Martin Luther."],[435.9,"I try to be nice."],[437.34,"So I'd like to have some sympathy for Martin Luther."],[440.06,"At least think he was doing the best he could."],[442.67,"And this is the approach that, well, on some of the specifics of this stuff, N.T."],[449.07,"Wright's hard to nail down."],[450.19,"I was about to say this is what N."],[451.31,"D. Wright does, but he's a little hard to nail down on this."],[454.67,"But he will often take the approach that basically Martin Luther was trying to use St."],[462.71,"Paul to answer quite theological questions that were important in the 16th century."],[470.09,"And I think that's a good sympathetic approach to take."],[473.05,"He's engaged in theological conflicts within, you know, essentially the Roman Catholic Church in Germany in the 16th century."],[481.77,"And he's weighing in on these various controversial issues."],[485.61,"And all this stuff like the Old Testament canon and stuff that people throw accusations at Protestants about, this stuff was still in motion at the time Martin Luther was alive, and they were having public debates on all this stuff."],[498.09,"So he's entering into that, and he's trying to read St."],[501.61,"Paul and say, how does what St"],[505.65,"Paul teaches apply to these questions we're dealing with now and these controversies?"],[511.09,"And he's doing that within the Western Christian theological tradition."],[515.82,"So he doesn't have access to like big collections of the Greek fathers."],[529.02,"And archaeology does not exist."],[531.44,"So there are no archaeological finds."],[533.68,"There's no data."],[536.13,"In that regard."],[538.45,"So the problem isn't so much then with Martin Luther himself and what he was trying to do."],[543.65,"We can say whether he did a good job or not."],[546.19,"In terms of whether whether he was applying St."],[550.59,"Paul correctly to his own time and place."],[584.93,"Because they were asking fundamentally different questions than we're dealing with now."],[590.38,"And so this is I don't know if this isn't going to sound like me being charitable, but uh I am actually trying to be charitable in that I think a uh the the the conservative churches of the Reformation, so your conservative Lutheran churches, your conservative Reformed churches, the ones that harken back to the Reformation, are essentially sort of early modern relics."],[619.04,"They've taken, and this is one of the perils of conservatism in general."],[623.36,"Perils of conservatism in any arena is that you take some particular era, some particular place in time, and you make that and you universalize it."],[635.08,"You say, this is the truth, like for all times and all places, not just this one time and this one place."],[645.29,"And that's dangerous because, of course, there is no particular time or place that is perfect and universal."],[651.05,"By definition, it's particular."],[658.09,"There are people in the Orthodox Church who take one particular era, 19th century Russia, some period of the Byzantine Empire."],[666.99,"Some particular era and historical form and cultural form that the church has taken in that place."],[673.51,"It said, this is what the Orthodox Church should look like everywhere, always, all the time."],[680.29,"And that's not how it works either."],[684.53,"So conservative is conservatism is important in a lot of ways, but that's the danger."],[691.09,"The danger is that you slip into that kind of golden age thinking of like this is the perfect, and we need to just now mirror this."],[699.1,"And recreate this."],[701.1,"Uh, this kind of LARPing cosplay way, and then we're really doing it, you know."],[706.14,"Um, so yeah, that was me trying to be charitable."],[710.06,"So I'm sorry to all of our Protestant friends who I'm now offended."],[713.26,"Um, but so basically, we've already we spent a lot of time uh, a couple years going through all of St."],[723.14,"Paul's epistles. Um, and I think we saw along the way how that reading of St."],[728.02,"Paul doesn't really work."],[730.23,"Um so what we're gonna try to do tonight when we get to uh when we go through this section of St."],[738.23,"James is take a reading of St."],[741.03,"James that makes sense with what St."],[743.83,"James is saying, but I think then we're gonna see that there's not really any conflict at all with what with what St."],[751.27,"Paul was saying."],[753.03,"And so we've sort of pulled out the two blocks at the bottom of the Jengo pile for the whole idea of like St."],[762.55,"James and Jewish Christianity and St."],[764.23,"and Paul Gentile Christianity."],[767.31,"So, you know, the whole the whole edifice falls apart."],[770.67,"So, like, I I couldn't go through this part of St."],[773.79,"James without addressing all that, because it's like the elephant in the room."],[777.41,"Especially because that has now become, even though Christian biblical scholarship has sort of moved past that now, um, both Jewish apologetics and Muslim apologetics have picked up that whole narrative from Adolf von Harnack and the 19th century or 19th century German friends and run with it."],[802.47,"To the point that, for example, Islamic apologists will say that Islam is the historical descendant of that St."],[810.67,"James Jewish Christianity, for example."],[814.53,"It's sort of attack Paul as being this phony."],[818.46,"Who tried to subvert Christianity."],[822.7,"And uh Jewish apologists basically just take Luther's reading of St."],[829.58,"Paul for granted."],[832.06,"That's what St."],[832.7,"Paul said. He said the law was abolished."],[835.02,"It's like, no, he said the exact opposite."],[837.66,"Um so even though, as I said, Christ Christian biblical studies has sort of moved past that now, because it keeps getting brought up in these other circles, apologetic circles, still have to kind of address it."],[854.92,"Um and um yeah, the last little piece then being if you're one of those people, even if you're one of the, you know, you're not going down the whole route of two different Christianities and yada yada yada."],[868.86,"If you're in a more traditional uh reformed or Lutheran denomination, basically you have to do one of two things."],[878.22,"You either have to sort of marginalize the text of Saint James, which Luther kind of literally did, pushed it into a margin, um, or you have to uh take kind of a really bizarre interpretation of the Epistle of St."],[896.0,"St. James, where basically you say, Well, when"],[896.32,"James says justification, it means something totally different than what St."],[896.56,"Paul means And when he says faith, he means something totally different than what St."],[906.04,"Paul means. To try and make them fit together."],[911.26,"Um, which again seems to me uh a bizarre choice when the other option is just like, hey, maybe we've misread St."],[919.18,"Paul. It's possible I've made an error."],[922.06,"Um, and unless you're heavily invested in the idea that 16th century Germany or Geneva or some other locale is the perfect expression of uh Christianity for all time, then we're able to reconsider these things."],[940.78,"And I think Calvin and Luther themselves would want us to reconsider these things rather than just sort of slavishly hold to things they wrote all that time ago."],[951.22,"I will say this for the Anglicans, they feel no need to hang on to the 39 articles in any serious sense, or to think that Henry VIII sort of perfectly nailed it."],[965.54,"So uh they they do have that going for them at least."],[970.58,"So um all that said by way of introduction."],[974.14,"Some of that was rehash of the introduction introduction."],[977.62,"Uh, but all I' saidve, unless there's any other questions, comments, concerns."],[984.94,"Late breaking news report.s, we'll go ahead and get started here in the Epistle of St"],[990.86,"James, chapter 2, verse 14."],[993.98,"What does it profit, my brethren, if someone says he has faith but does not have works?"],[999.98,"Can faith save him?"],[1003.26,"Well, there you go."],[1004.78,"Non-controversial. Um but so um notice that it's someone says he has faith."],[1018.15,"But does not have works."],[1020.35,"Um, and there's a there's a a uh little bit of a problem with the uh New King James translation there at the end of 14, uh that we'll talk about here in a second."],[1031.23,"But so we've talked about how faith, when it's used here, and we've seen this already in St."],[1036.99,"James Epistle, you see the word faith and the word belief and believe, these are all the same Greek word, and a much better translation is faithfulness."],[1046.45,"That that conveys much better the idea in English."],[1050.07,"Because when we think of faith, we think about an intellectual act."],[1054.65,"Putting the T on the true-false test."],[1057.31,"Belief. But that's not how it's typically used."],[1061.79,"We've seen how it's contrasted with, for example, falling away."],[1066.56,"And intellectually believing something is true and falling away are not opposites."],[1071.68,"Um, faithfulness or loyalty"],[1074.98,"Allegiance versus falling away are opposites."],[1080.5,"So if we read this that way, what does it profit, my brethren, if someone says he is faithful but does not have works?"],[1090.42,"Does not do anything."],[1092.87,"That makes perfect sense."],[1094.77,"Because if I say I'm faithful and devoted to my wife, but I never show that by what I do."],[1101.49,"I don't take out the trash."],[1103.51,"I don't help her when she needs, I don't."],[1107.39,"Is anybody gonna believe?"],[1109.89,"Does that faithfulness, have any value?"],[1114.09,"No. And so when I say it's a bad translation, is they left out, there's a Greek article in that second clause."],[1125.45,"What it literally says is, can this faith save him?"],[1128.81,"Can this faithfulness save him?"],[1130.49,"This kind. Will he find salvation with that kind of faithfulness?"],[1134.99,"Where he just goes around saying he's faithful to God."],[1138.04,"Now remember last time we saw the reference that St."],[1141.96,"Paul also made in Romans, that it's the not the hearers of the law, the hearers of the Torah, but the doers that are justified."],[1150.46,"They're the ones who."],[1152.16,"And so this is continuing that idea."],[1154.82,"It's very easy to say."],[1156.02,"Oh, yeah, I'm faithful to God, I'm devoted, I love God"],[1161.24,"But that would normally show itself."],[1165.14,"And the way that's normally talked about in the gospels is the idea of fruit."],[1168.92,"A tree produces fruit."],[1170.2,"It doesn't choose to produce fruit."],[1173.08,"If it's alive and it's vital and it's right, then it produces fruit."],[1181.45,"So verse 15."],[1183.13,"Those two sort of rhetorical questions."],[1185.61,"If a brother or sister is naked and destitute of daily food, and one of you says to them, Depart in peace, be warmed and filled."],[1194.09,"But you do not give them the things which are needed for the body, what does it profit?"],[1201.58,"So this is that talk is cheap."],[1204.47,"You've got a picture of somebody shivering in the cold, you know, and they come to ask you for help, and you say, Hey, may God, you know, feed you."],[1213.35,"Keep warm. Have a great day."],[1216.65,"This is a comparison."],[1218.01,"That's what saying you're faithful and loyal and devoted, but then not doing it is like."],[1222.83,"Does it accomplish anything?"],[1225.07,"Does it produce anything?"],[1227.55,"Verse 17: Thus also, faith or faithfulness by itself, if it does not have works, is dead."],[1236.53,"Tree doesn't produce any fruit year after year."],[1238.77,"You conclude eventually the tree's dead."],[1239.81,"That's a pretty good sign"],[1247.67,"Verse 18, but someone will say, You have faith and I have works."],[1258.2,"So he's saying, well, some people are just really dedicated and pious."],[1262.84,"And faithfully, we were talking about piety last time."],[1265.34,"People who say they're religious and they're pious, but then, you know."],[1269.9,"Same kind of thing."],[1270.64,"You see, this is a person."],[1272.32,"But I'm I'm out there, I'm active."],[1274.0,"I'm out there doing things."],[1276.26,"Like these are two separate things."],[1278.36,"That's what he's addressing."],[1281.48,"Show me your faith without your works, and I will show you my faith by my works."],[1286.37,"So show me your faithfulness without your works."],[1290.93,"How are you gonna do that?"],[1294.13,"Show me your faithful to your wife and love your wife."],[1300.45,"Without pointing to anything you do."],[1304.94,"He's saying, I'll show you that I'm faithful by what I do."],[1308.08,"You'll be able to look and see and know that I'm I'm faithful."],[1314.24,"Verse 19. You believe that there is one God, you do well."],[1319.2,"Even the demons believe and tremble."],[1322.64,"So this is this is the problem with the whole true false test view of faith."],[1324.48,"whole true false. The test view"],[1331.53,"Because if we gave the true false test about Christian doctrine, every demon would get a hundred percent."],[1342.7,"They know about the Trinity."],[1345.34,"They know that Christ is God and man."],[1348.91,"They know all these things."],[1350.67,"They know more than we do."],[1354.24,"So if that was what it meant, if just assenting to the truth of certain propositions, if that's what faith meant."],[1363.38,"If that's what faithfulness was, that's what this word meant, then all the demons in hell would be saved."],[1373.31,"That and tremble, is because, why?"],[1377.13,"Well, because knowing all those things doesn't change who they are before God."],[1383.45,"Does it change them?"],[1384.89,"Does it change their relationship with God?"],[1386.81,"Just knowing those things."],[1389.14,"They're still in terror of God because of what they've become."],[1395.19,"So, rolling back a few verses, remember the question, can this kind of faith save a person?"],[1404.79,"Well, it can't save a demon."],[1407.11,"That means it can't save a person."],[1409.69,"But this demon example gives us an idea of what Saint James means by salvation."],[1417.39,"What does he mean by salvation?"],[1419.87,"What is it that that kind of faith fails to do for the demon?"],[1425.63,"Fails to change anything."],[1428.21,"Fails to change anything."],[1430.05,"So salvation then for Saint James means and is some kind of actual transformation."],[1439.34,"It's some kind of actual transformation."],[1442.88,"Because if the way, if, for example, we were a person who believed that faith was ascent."],[1451.52,"Was a scent to certain doctrinal truths"],[1455.94,"And if you believe that, that's it, you're saved."],[1459.3,"And what we meant by saved was not punished for the evil you've done."],[1465.38,"Let's say we were such a person."],[1468.26,"I'm not going to say who that might be."],[1472.18,"You can identify that for yourself."],[1474.02,"But if we believe that, then this demon example wouldn't make sense."],[1481.78,"Why couldn't the demons be let off the hook?"],[1486.42,"If that was how faith and salvation work."],[1490.95,"But so what St."],[1492.63,"James is saying here is that without the works, without the fruit."],[1496.25,"That those fruit are part and parcel of the transformation."],[1503.05,"Part and parcel of the transformation of a human person."],[1508.05,"Demons, likewise, don't have the works."],[1512.95,"Everything they do is evil."],[1516.28,"And so that doesn't help them."],[1519.13,"So remember what we talked about with St."],[1521.45,"Paul over and over again."],[1522.81,"That we saw in Philippians, we saw in Romans, we saw that St."],[1525.77,"Paul is always talking about what we called synergy."],[1528.95,"God is working in the world."],[1530.63,"This is what in Orthodox theology we call the divine energies."],[1534.27,"God is working in the creation."],[1536.99,"He's pouring out his love, he's bringing about justice."],[1541.04,"He's bringing about peace."],[1542.98,"He's doing this."],[1544.1,"And we as human beings can then act in tandem with that."],[1550.26,"We can cooperate in the original sense."],[1552.9,"We can cooperate with that."],[1554.42,"We can participate in that."],[1556.98,"And that means being the means through which God does it."],[1562.6,"So God loves this person who's standing in front of me."],[1569.24,"If I become the vehicle through which God expresses his love for this person, that's not just going to help this person, but it's going to transform and change me."],[1581.68,"And this is why the example St."],[1583.52,"James uses here is so pertinent."],[1585.28,"When that person comes to me, remember we talked about last time how St."],[1590.72,"James's epistle and Christ's teaching in St."],[1593.6,"Matthew's Gospel dovetail so often."],[1597.04,"Remember the parable of the sheep and the goats?"],[1601.26,"Sheep go to heaven, goats go to hell."],[1604.46,"Not because one of them believed a list of doctrines and the other one didn't."],[1609.9,"Based on what they did."],[1611.6,"So when this person stands in front of you and needs clothing and food and shelter, and you give it to them, it's not that you're earning credits with God towards salvation and, once you reach a certain number of credits, you get saved."],[1626.07,"Martin Luther was right to reject that kind of idea."],[1630.68,"It's that you become the vehicle through which God is loving this person and clothing this person and healing them."],[1637.4,"Because God works through means."],[1639.48,"He doesn't have to."],[1641.56,"God could do everything directly."],[1643.48,"God didn't need to create us, he didn't need to create the world."],[1646.3,"He did this out of love."],[1647.9,"And part of his love is that he chooses to work through us, not just through our actions, but through our prayers."],[1656.16,"Through the prayers of the saints."],[1657.53,"He chooses to work through us and work through means to allow us to participate in what he's doing in the world, to transform us."],[1665.05,"us So that we can find salvation and he can share his life with eternally."],[1665.37,"That's what St"],[1670.91,"James is talking about."],[1672.05,"So the demons don't are our exhibit A of not ever in any way participating in what God is doing in the world."],[1680.39,"Resisting it at all points."],[1682.41,"Fighting against it, rebelling against it."],[1685.01,"As hard as you can."],[1686.39,"And so St."],[1687.03,"James is saying the person who says, oh yeah, I know all that stuff, the person who hears the Torah, the person who's memorized the Torah."],[1694.53,"Knows the law of God, knows Christian doctrine backwards and forwards, can recite the Nicene Creed, can tell you about all the canons of the ecumenical councils."],[1703.59,"But there's no transformation in their life."],[1705.91,"They're not cooperating, participating in what God is doing in the world"],[1710.49,"That means they're not being transformed."],[1715.53,"What did we see was the end game of salvation for Saint Paul was becoming sons of God."],[1722.25,"He says it over and over again in Romans."],[1726.09,"Over and over again, that we're becoming sons of God."],[1729.06,"Like the angelic beings."],[1730.76,"That's that transformation is happening."],[1733.08,"So St. James is working with the same view of salvation, same view of faithfulness as Saint Paul."],[1741.06,"If you dig in a little to what he's saying here."],[1743.88,"And his whole argument here falls apart if you don't have that understanding of faithfulness and that understanding of salvation."],[1749.96,"If you try and plug in a different one, done work"],[1756.48,"That tells you, well, maybe I'm defining these terms wrong."],[1760.96,"In general. Not just St."],[1762.82,"James uses words totally differently than everybody else."],[1766.1,"That's not really a logical conclusion to jump to."],[1787.54,"Channeling Mr. T for a moment, I guess."],[1790.02,"Um faith without works is dead"],[1795.34,"Verse 21, was not Abraham our father justified by works when he offered Isaac his son on the altar?"],[1804.54,"Do you see that faith was working together with his works?"],[1808.14,"And by works, faith was made perfect."],[1812.94,"Verse 23, and the scripture was fulfilled, which says, Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness."],[1820.22,"And he was called the friend of God."],[1823.98,"So when you read most Protestant Roman Catholic treatments of this piece, it's an argument about when was Abraham justified?"],[1837.58,"To use common American terminology, when did Abraham get saved?"],[1841.71,"What point in his life?"],[1843.36,"And you get lots of different tacks taken, to go through this."],[1847.0,"Because it's all about getting saved."],[1849.9,"And so uh Roman Catholic commentators frequently point out well, look, St."],[1855.58,"Paul refers to another another time when Abraham was justified by his faithfulness, so or his faith."],[1863.42,"Therefore, look, he's justified multiple times."],[1865.74,"So you get that approach where they're like, oh, see, justification is this ongoing thing."],[1869.83,"And so you get saved, you know, later on, and all of that."],[1874.79,"Um most conservative Protestants will take attack where they're like, well, he means something totally different by justification."],[1881.43,"Paul does Uh that's here than what St."],[1887.23,"Uh you get Protestants on the other end who will say more like, well, they just disagree."],[1892.45,"They just, you know, St."],[1893.65,"Paul says he was justified at this point, and St."],[1895.97,"James says he was justified at that point."],[1898.18,"So the problem here is a broader one."],[1903.38,"Uh, and it has to do with that whole concept again of salvation."],[1909.84,"And especially with this idea of merit."],[1914.1,"So you may have heard Orthodox people say that Protestants and Roman Catholics have more in common with each other than either of them do with Orthodox. theology"],[1926.38,"This is a place where that's not just a rhetorical thing."],[1930.98,"Because in both your classical Roman Catholic understanding of salvation, and I say classical Roman Catholic because I know what, you know, it's all over the place now."],[1942.92,"But classically. Your Roman Catholic view of salvation is that there is sort of this bar where you're at salvation."],[1952.68,"Where you go to heaven instead of hell."],[1955.26,"Where you get eternal life instead of eternal punishment."],[1958.62,"There's this bar."],[1960.54,"And you can actually go over it."],[1962.31,"In the Roman Cassius Roman Catholic system, the saints have extra merit past what they needed for their own salvation."],[1966.87,"Pope can dispense it"],[1970.11,"That goes into the treasury."],[1974.43,"Right. Saints themselves can cash it in for miracles."],[1977.71,"I'm trying not to be overly crude and crass about this."],[1980.59,"I'm trying. But that's that's the general idea."],[1985.23,"And so you sort of fill up to this bar through this combination of faith and works."],[1992.37,"So they'll say salvation is by faith and works in that sense."],[1995.73,"That both of those contribute merit."],[1999.11,"And then you can even break it down."],[2000.55,"There's condyn merit and congruent merit."],[2002.31,"It gets really complicated."],[2003.29,"In the medieval system, especially."],[2004.91,"But um, but the idea is that you get up to this bar."],[2010.28,"So classical Protestantism, and again, contemporary Protestantism is all over the place."],[2017.26,"Especially since now, you know, the word Protestant used to refer to a particular group of churches that came out of the Reformation, and now people just use the word Protestant to mean not Roman Catholic or Orthodox and call themselves Christians."],[2031.15,"So um it's because catch, okay."],[2033.39,"So all over the place."],[2034.35,"But classically, if you're talking about the Protestant Reformation."],[2037.44,"Classically, they did not reject that whole paradigm."],[2041.98,"But they said, uh, you as a sinner are unable to get have any merit before God."],[2051.11,"Pull verse out of Isaiah, out of context."],[2052.71,"You can't do anything meritorious in the sight of God"],[2053.47,"All our works are as filthy rags."],[2055.49,"Calvin, total depravity."],[2059.47,"Luther's snow-covered dunghill."],[2062.09,"Like, that's it."],[2064.49,"Um, but Christ, through his life of obedience, his active obedience throughout his life, accrued this infinite amount of merit."],[2077.05,"And Christ's merit gets wholesale deposited in your account when you believe."],[2084.99,"When you believe in Christ, total balance transfer."],[2090.88,"And all the sin on your account, all the demerit on your account gets transferred over to him. and he's punished for it on the cross"],[2098.12,"That's sort of your classical Protestant formulation for how that works."],[2105.64,"But what both of those have in common is that you have you're saved by merit, and that merit comes from keeping the law, keeping the Torah."],[2119.0,"So we've already seen as we were going through St."],[2121.08,"Paul's epistles, one big problem with that, and that that's not how St."],[2125.88,"Paul thinks the law works."],[2128.54,"The Torah works."],[2129.5,"Not let that it doesn't work that way anymore."],[2132.14,"He thought it never worked that way."],[2133.82,"We talked about how the Torah was sort of, as St."],[2136.22,"Paul describes it, was this sort of sin management system."],[2140.6,"That was put in place until Christ came to deal with sin and death and the devil."],[2147.26,"But to kind of manage it within Israel so that God could continue to dwell in the midst of his people who were sinful."],[2156.22,"So we had to manage it until Christ came to deal with it."],[2159.98,"That's how he sees it."],[2161.34,"So there was never anything about accruing any kind of merit or favor with God by not eating pork."],[2170.04,"Or by not murdering people."],[2174.09,"Or whatever you want it, not wearing tassels on your garbage, whatever you want to say."],[2178.67,"Um correctly performing the Day of Atonement ritual."],[2183.33,"God was not like, well, here's your gold star sticker for today, but you get enough gold stars, heaven."],[2190.15,"That was never how it worked for St."],[2192.07,"Paul. So that's one problem."],[2195.66,"But this whole merit concept is another problem."],[2199.87,"Because if we're talking about salvation the way St."],[2202.11,"Paul and St."],[2202.75,"James talk about it, if salvation is transformation."],[2210.24,"If justification being set right, being set back in order, sanctification becoming holy is sharing in God's holiness."],[2217.82,"Glorification is coming to share God shares his glory with us, in this transformation, and that whole merit. thing doesn't really work"],[2227.88,"You can't go and bring half the price of a TV to a Walmart and get half a TV."],[2236.25,"The banking thing falls apart."],[2237.87,"So there's if it's transformation, then we have to totally abandon that paradigm."],[2244.89,"So this is why it drives me up a tree when I see Orthodox people say that we believe we're saved by faith and works."],[2255.14,"But faithfulness, consists of accepting the truth of certain things."],[2264.04,"And doing living your life in a certain way."],[2267.5,"Because part of living your life is how you see things and how you think about things and how you view things."],[2275.02,"And how we think about things is active."],[2281.9,"It's not a one-time action of I accept that this is true."],[2285.82,"Oh no, now I doubt that that is true."],[2289.42,"It is an ongoing submission of our thoughts, our way of viewing the world to God."],[2299.58,"As it says in Proverbs, there's a way that seems right to a man, and its end is destruction."],[2306.46,"And so on a day-to-day basis, we have to subject this is the way I this is what I think I want."],[2312.66,"This is how I want to look at this person."],[2315.46,"This person is my enemy."],[2317.68,"This person was rude to me."],[2318.88,"I want revenge on this person."],[2320.24,"I'm bitter toward the right."],[2321.76,"How we view events in the world, how we view these things."],[2324.8,"We have all these motivations, do things in other ways."],[2326.88,"We have to continually submit that, our understanding, to how God tells us things are."],[2339.64,"Even belief, if we're just going to talk about belief."],[2343.13,"Belief is not, well, I've done a lot of reading and I've done a lot of thinking, and I've decided that what uh God says through his church is true."],[2352.97,"Where I'm the judge, I'm still the judge of what's true and false."],[2357.13,"I've just come around to deciding that God is right about things."],[2361.81,"That's not how belief works."],[2365.11,"Belief is the opposite."],[2367.19,"Belief is I don't understand."],[2369.59,"I accept that I don't understand."],[2371.35,"I accept that I can't understand."],[2374.15,"I accept that I'm sinful and I want to twist things around to my advantage and to think that they're the way I want them to be and not the way they really are."],[2384.87,"But I'm going to submit to what God says, even when I really don't like it."],[2391.21,"In and of myself."],[2393.37,"So even that is an active, ongoing process."],[2395.85,"ing. Of be, as St"],[2400.27,"Paul says, transformed by the renewing of our mind, our noose."],[2404.69,"The way we see and understand reality."],[2409.73,"And so there's the transformation of the mind, and there's a transformation of the way we live our lives."],[2416.29,"And even physically."],[2417.9,"We've talked before about how if you've seen someone who's lived a hard life and who's gone down the wrong path, you can see that manifest itself in their physical appearance."],[2427.34,"And vice versa, when you see someone repent and you see someone's life transform, you can see that in their physical appearance."],[2435.34,"Either way Because this is a total transformation."],[2438.38,"Either way. Paul and St"],[2441.06,"Salvation encompasses all of this."],[2443.7,"So that's how both St."],[2445.62,"James are talking about salvation."],[2448.74,"So if we have that kind of idea in mind, that talking about Abraham in this way is not a problem."],[2455.96,"We don't have to figure out where's the moment in Abraham's life where he stopped being on his way to hell and started being on his way to heaven."],[2465.16,"Because that whole that doesn't make sense anymore."],[2469.88,"We have this variety of moments in Abraham's life where he's."],[2473.02,"What was the command given to Abraham by God?"],[2475.22,"Walk before me and be righteous."],[2478.68,"So we have these moments in his walk, and we see over the course of Abraham's life him being transformed."],[2484.36,"He starts out in one place in Ur, your sort of typical Sumerian"],[2492.48,"And then by the end of his life, he's become something very different."],[2498.08,"And someone very different."],[2500.56,"This is why we read Saints' lives."],[2505.76,"St. Mary of Egypt starts out in one place."],[2509.58,"I was about to say basically a prostitute, but she actually says she was worse than a prostitute because at least a prostitute is doing it for the money."],[2516.86,"And she wasn't."],[2518.67,"And then at the end of her life has become this like a beacon of holiness."],[2523.17,"Through her repentance."],[2525.37,"We see this with all the saints, this transformation."],[2527.45,"And so St"],[2532.13,"Paul could talk about one moment in Abraham's life in that journey."],[2536.69,"And St. James could talk about another moment in that journey."],[2540.87,"And how Abraham is being transformed at all of those moments."],[2544.31,"And those don't contradict each other."],[2547.27,"Because the transformation isn't happening in God."],[2552.31,"It's not God was angry at me and now he's happy with me."],[2558.55,"And it's especially not God was angry with me, but now when he looks at me, he sees Jesus and not me."],[2564.79,"If he saw me, he would still hate me."],[2569.83,"But he loves Jesus."],[2571.59,"And so I sneak in like a Trojan horse past his wrath."],[2578.64,"Nah. That's not it."],[2581.74,"Change isn't happening to God."],[2583.1,"God doesn't need to change."],[2584.22,"God's perfect. God's holy."],[2585.75,"We need to change."],[2586.71,"Change is happening in Abraham."],[2589.43,"So what do we see reading it from that perspective?"],[2597.24,"Abraham. Was Abraham our father justified?"],[2599.8,"And we're talking about justification."],[2601.8,"Think about the other way we use that term when you're doing word processing."],[2608.2,"Justification is setting things in order."],[2610.86,"If it's right justified, it's all lined up on the right side."],[2614.42,"Left justified, all lined up on the left side."],[2616.42,"Full justified, all lined up down the page."],[2619.83,"So was Abraham, our father, justified?"],[2622.55,"Was he set"],[2623.21,"Was he transformed?"],[2626.25,"Did he become more like God by works, by what he did when he offered Isaac his son on the altar?"],[2637.61,"Would it have been enough if he just sat there at the camp and said, Oh yeah, God, I'd be willing to sacrifice my son?"],[2642.57,"I totally would."],[2643.53,"Yeah. No. He went and he was willing to go through with it."],[2652.24,"You see that faith was working together with his works."],[2656.38,"His faithfulness. His works working together."],[2662.24,"And by works, his faithfulness was made perfect."],[2664.8,"We talk about perfect."],[2665.68,"We think about perfect as no sins."],[2667.53,"Perfect is the idea here is complete."],[2672.03,"So his faithfulness was made complete by him actually going and doing it."],[2679.65,"Him actually going and being willing to do it."],[2684.45,"So again, Abraham believed God."],[2686.75,"So it's the same word in Greek."],[2690.11,"Abraham was faithful to God."],[2692.43,"And it was accounted to him for righteousness."],[2696.53,"And that accounted."],[2697.33,"Again, this is not talking about like an accountant"],[2700.94,"Jules isn't here, but like an accountant."],[2703.76,"Adding up the amount of merit."],[2705.52,"Let's look at the balance sheet."],[2707.52,"Merit and demerit, merit and sin."],[2709.78,"That's not what it's talking about."],[2714.18,"It was accounted to him."],[2715.38,"The share he received was righteousness."],[2717.62,"He was transformed, he was justified."],[2720.07,"Because he was faithful."],[2723.16,"Now, again, we're not looking for this one moment."],[2727.09,"When he's justified."],[2728.05,"So St. James could have equally well said, was not Abraham justified by works when he left Ur to go and travel to an unknown land?"],[2742.42,"Because the correct response for Abram, when Yahweh the God of Israel came to him and said, Go and leave, and I'm going to give you this land, was not, Oh, I believe you."],[2753.12,"It was to get up and go."],[2755.22,"Not sit there in Ur and assent to, yes, I believe it is true, you will give my descendants that land."],[2762.42,"So he could have done that."],[2763.78,"He could have used any number of examples from Abraham's life."],[2769.94,"So this is not an argument between him and Paul about when the moment of justification was, because there is no moment of justification."],[2781.38,"And then, of course.se, the completely uncontroversial ver 24"],[2784.94,"You see then that a man is justified by works, and not by, and the New King James is trying to slip this past you."],[2791.18,"It says, not by faith alone."],[2797.58,"Don't match this."],[2799.02,"So this is the only place where the phrase by faith alone occurs in the New Testament."],[2805.02,"Even though Martin Luther added the word alone to Romans 3."],[2810.78,"It's the only place, and it has not by in front of it."],[2814.24,"But this doesn't mean, as our Roman Catholic friends would assert, that you're saved by this like combo of faith and works."],[2822.89,"They get piled up."],[2824.59,"This means that. you're saved by you're justified"],[2830.07,"You're set right by works by doing."],[2834.57,"Not just by faithfulness in the sense of claiming loyalty."],[2839.82,"To God. Verse 25, likewise, was not Rahab the harlot also justified by works."],[2846.86,"When she received the messengers and sent them out another way."],[2852.22,"This gets less talked about."],[2853.58,"Everybody argues about Abraham, but this is less."],[2855.66,"But this is the parallel example."],[2858.06,"Okay. Rahab was a harlot."],[2862.3,"This isn't just a pejorative."],[2864.3,"She was a prostitute."],[2865.42,"That's what she did for a living."],[2867.31,"Um. So she was some messages that sent them out another way"],[2872.87,"Did she continue being a prostitute?"],[2877.91,"No. It was not just like, oh, you did this nice thing for some Jews."],[2884.24,"Therefore, you are saved now."],[2891.97,"What was happening."],[2893.75,"Go back and we talked about this too."],[2895.43,"This is one of the big problems with interpreting the New Testament the way it reads the Old Testament."],[2900.55,"Is that we don't go back and actually read the Old Testament story."],[2904.25,"We treat it like it's some kind of weird proof text."],[2906.75,"What's going on in that story?"],[2909.01,"Rahab, when they show up, has heard about all the stuff Yahweh, the God of Israel did in Egypt, the plagues and all that."],[2915.3,"And all the stuff that happened in the wilderness."],[2917.62,"And because of that, she kind of fears the God of Israel."],[2923.14,"Yeah. And she's decided to switch sides."],[2929.46,"She has decided to switch sides."],[2933.7,"She said, You guys are coming in here, you are going to win because your God is greater and more powerful than the gods that rule around this place."],[2941.96,"And so that's why."],[2945.05,"Her loyalties. Faithfulness."],[2948.99,"She has switched loyalties, and that finds its expression in what?"],[2952.91,"In what she does?"],[2955.47,"In helping the spies, helping shelter the spies."],[2960.37,"And then what happens because of that?"],[2963.17,"She's justified in that when she dies, she's gonna go to heaven because she did that one nice thing."],[2967.82,"No. That then transforms her life."],[2971.98,"She becomes an Israelite."],[2974.62,"She joins the Israelites."],[2976.38,"She ends up in the genealogy of Christ that we read a couple weeks ago at Liturgy."],[2984.08,"Because she changed her loyalties to the God of Israel and took action."],[2987.92,"Took this action."],[2989.2,"That's what St."],[2990.0,"James is pointing to."],[2991.44,"That is what transformed her life."],[2993.36,"So it's like St."],[2994.32,"Mary of Egypt, then."],[2995.64,"Yeah. That is what transformed her life."],[2998.52,"Because that's what he's talking about with salvation and justification here."],[3002.04,"The transformation of life."],[3005.82,"And that's what happens with Rahab."],[3011.5,"Verse 26 for as the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is dead also."],[3019.58,"This idea of faithfulness without it being expressed in any kind of works."],[3023.26,"That's like a body without a soul."],[3025.58,"It's just an object."],[3027.26,"It's just a thing."],[3028.86,"There's no life to it."],[3031.15,"And what what what is life?"],[3035.15,"Life is, yeah."],[3036.51,"And it's yeah, it' its's. a it's a growth and transformation"],[3043.33,"If something's not growing and not changing at all, if something is inert, it is not alive."],[3050.21,"By definition. Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3059.81,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3065.17,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.64,"lv":2,"h":"James 2:14ff - why it's controversial","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up <b>James 2:14</b> - \"completely non-controversial passage\" (sarcasm)</li><li class=\"d0\">Nothing shocking or weird in the text itself</li><li class=\"d1\">controversy = <b>since Martin Luther</b>, a certain reading of <b>St. Paul's Epistles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">vs. the \"sort of plain meaning\" of James 2, second half</li><li class=\"d0\">Creates a <b>dilemma</b> for those convinced of that reading</li><li class=\"d0\">Option 1: <b>maybe we've read Paul incorrectly</b></li><li class=\"d1\">minority approach taken</li><li class=\"d1\">including by Luther himself - though ambiguous; his <b>James commentary</b> not as clear \"as I'm about to make it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"oversimplification. Don't @ me, Lutherans\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Option 2 (much more common): <b>James and Paul are opposed</b></li></ul>"},{"t":116.32,"lv":3,"h":"Two Christianities narrative","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">German scholarship, <b>18th century</b> onward: no longer beholden to Bible being true → easy to say \"yeah, they disagree\"</li><li class=\"d0\">James says opposite of Paul (<b>Galatians, Romans</b>) → basis for <b>two different Christianities</b> in early church</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Pauline Christianity</b>: mainly Gentiles, increasingly caricatured</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul = anti-Jewish, hates Torah, tells everyone to stop keeping kosher - \"almost a Gnostic\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>James</b>: barely a Christian; Jewish leader, brother was \"a good rabbi\", Christ not divine</li><li class=\"d1\">leading <b>Jewish Christianity</b>; epistle \"shoehorned\" into NT because he was prominent</li><li class=\"d0\">Makes <b>zero historical sense</b> at formation of NT canon</li><li class=\"d1\">2nd-3rd c.: communities worldwide, mostly <b>Gentile</b>, using both Paul and James <b>authoritatively</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no sense of conflict</li><li class=\"d0\">Presupposition: they flatly contradicted, <b>nobody noticed until the 16th century</b></li></ul>"},{"t":265.73,"lv":3,"h":"Nobody noticed until Luther","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"When I say nobody noticed it, I mean <b>nobody</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Recent published <b>dissertation</b>: every church father source to <b>AD 300</b> on <b>works of the law</b> in Paul</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Not one\" understands Paul the way <b>Luther</b> did</li><li class=\"d0\">Extra presupposition: those closest to Paul - <b>Gentile churches he founded</b> - all <b>completely misunderstood</b> plain meaning</li><li class=\"d1\">Luther \"figured it out\" in <b>Germany, 16th c.</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Some <b>Reformed</b> people say it outright: Pauline gospel <b>lost by end of 1st c.</b>, <b>recovered by Luther</b></li><li class=\"d1\">give credit for being <b>consistent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">position you're <b>forced to</b> by historical evidence if Luther read it right</li></ul>"},{"t":428.78,"lv":2,"h":"Being charitable to Luther","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Another option for those with <b>sympathy for Luther</b> - \"I try to be nice\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>N.T. Wright</b> - \"a little hard to nail down\" - often takes this approach</li><li class=\"d1\">Luther using Paul to answer <b>theological questions of the 16th c.</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Context: conflicts within <b>Roman Catholic Church in Germany</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT canon etc. still <b>in motion</b>, public debates</li><li class=\"d1\">Western tradition only: no big collections of <b>Greek fathers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">barely just got the <b>Greek NT</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no archaeology</b>, no data</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem isn't Luther himself - whether he applied Paul well to his time is a <b>historical question</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No reason a <b>21st c. American</b> is beholden to how he applied it to <b>16th c. Germany</b> - \"fundamentally different questions\"</li></ul>"},{"t":590.38,"lv":3,"h":"Danger of conservatism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Trying to be charitable: <b>conservative Lutheran/Reformed</b> churches = \"early modern relics\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Peril of conservatism</b> in any arena</li><li class=\"d1\">take one era/place, <b>universalize</b> it - \"truth for all times and places\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no time or place is perfect - by definition <b>particular</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same in Orthodoxy</li><li class=\"d1\">some pick <b>19th c. Russia</b>, a period of the <b>Byzantine Empire</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"this is what the Church should look like everywhere, always\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Danger = <b>golden age thinking</b>; \"LARPing cosplay\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Apologizes to Protestant friends \"I've now offended\"</li></ul>"},{"t":753.03,"lv":2,"h":"Why address this: Paul, apologetics","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Spent <b>a couple years</b> in Paul's epistles - that reading \"doesn't really work\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Tonight: read <b>James</b> as it makes sense → <b>no conflict with Paul</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pulls out \"two blocks at the bottom of the <b>Jenga</b> pile\" - James/Jewish Christianity vs. Paul/Gentile Christianity → edifice falls</li><li class=\"d0\">Had to address - \"<b>elephant in the room</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christian biblical scholarship has <b>moved past</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>Jewish and Muslim apologetics</b> picked up narrative from <b>Adolf von Harnack</b> and 19th c. Germans</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Islamic</b> apologists: Islam = historical descendant of <b>James's Jewish Christianity</b>; Paul = \"phony\" who subverted Christianity</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jewish</b> apologists take <b>Luther's reading</b> of Paul for granted: \"law abolished\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"No, he said the exact opposite\"</li></ul>"},{"t":854.92,"lv":3,"h":"Options for Lutheran/Reformed readers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Traditional Reformed/Lutheran: <b>one of two things</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>marginalize</b> James - Luther \"pushed it into a margin\"</li><li class=\"d1\">or \"really bizarre\" reading: <b>justification</b> and <b>faith</b> mean something totally different than in Paul</li><li class=\"d0\">Bizarre when the other option is \"hey, <b>maybe we've misread Paul</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Possible unless heavily invested in <b>16th c. Germany/Geneva</b> as perfect expression for all time</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Calvin and Luther</b> themselves would want us to reconsider - not \"slavishly hold\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Anglicans</b>: no need to hang on to the <b>39 Articles</b>, or think <b>Henry VIII</b> \"perfectly nailed it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Questions, comments, \"late breaking news reports\"? → on to text</li></ul>"},{"t":984.94,"lv":2,"h":"James 2:14 - can this faith save?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:14</b>: \"What does it profit, my brethren, if someone says he has faith but does not have works, can faith save him?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem with <b>NKJV</b> at end of 14</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Faith / belief / believe</b> = same Greek word; better: <b>faithfulness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we hear \"faith\" as intellectual act - \"putting the <b>T on the true-false test</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">contrasted with <b>falling away</b> - not opposites of intellectual belief</li><li class=\"d1\">opposite of falling away = <b>faithfulness, loyalty, allegiance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reads: \"if someone says he is <b>faithful</b> but does not have works\"</li><li class=\"d1\">say I'm devoted to my wife but never <b>take out the trash</b>, never help → does it have <b>any value</b>? \"No\"</li><li class=\"d0\">NKJV omits <b>Greek article</b> in second clause</li><li class=\"d1\">literally: <b>\"can THIS faith save him?\"</b> - this <b>kind</b>, saying he's faithful to God</li></ul>"},{"t":1181.45,"lv":2,"h":"James 2:15-17 - talk is cheap","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Continues idea from last time: <b>Romans</b> - not hearers of the Torah but <b>doers</b> justified</li><li class=\"d0\">Easy to say \"I'm faithful, I love God\" - normally <b>shows itself</b></li><li class=\"d1\">gospels' word: <b>fruit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tree \"doesn't choose\" to produce fruit; if <b>alive and vital</b> it does</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:15-16</b>: naked, destitute of daily food; \"depart in peace, be warmed and filled\" but no needed things → \"what does it profit?\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>talk is cheap</b>: person shivering, you say \"may God feed you, keep warm, have a great day\"</li><li class=\"d1\">saying you're faithful but not doing it = same → \"doesn't accomplish anything\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:17</b>: faithfulness by itself without works is <b>dead</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tree no fruit year after year → \"eventually the tree's <b>dead</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1247.67,"lv":2,"h":"James 2:18 - show me","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:18</b>: \"You have faith and I have works\"</li><li class=\"d1\">some are just dedicated, pious, faithful - <b>piety</b> from last time</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm out there, I'm active\" - treats as <b>two separate things</b>; that's what he's addressing</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Show me your faith without your works, and I will show you my faith by my works\"</li><li class=\"d1\">show me you're <b>faithful to your wife</b> without pointing to anything you do - \"how are you going to do that?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'll show you I'm faithful by what I <b>do</b>\" → you can look and see</li></ul>"},{"t":1314.24,"lv":2,"h":"James 2:19 - demons believe","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:19</b>: believe there is one God, you do well; \"even the <b>demons</b> believe and tremble\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem with the <b>true-false test view of faith</b></li><li class=\"d1\">on a test of Christian doctrine <b>every demon gets 100%</b></li><li class=\"d1\">know the <b>Trinity</b>, Christ <b>God and man</b> - \"know more than we do\"</li><li class=\"d0\">If faith = assenting to propositions → <b>all the demons in hell would be saved</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Tremble\" - why?</li><li class=\"d1\">knowing doesn't change <b>who they are before God</b> or their relationship with him</li><li class=\"d1\">\"still in terror of God because of what they've <b>become</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Rolling back: can this kind of faith save a person? → can't save a demon → <b>can't save a person</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1430.05,"lv":3,"h":"What James means by salvation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Demon example shows what <b>salvation</b> means for James</li><li class=\"d1\">what that faith fails to do for the demon: <b>fails to change anything</b></li><li class=\"d1\">salvation = <b>actual transformation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">If faith = <b>assent to doctrinal truths</b>, saved = <b>not punished for evil done</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm not going to say who that might be\"</li><li class=\"d1\">then demon example makes no sense - why couldn't demons be <b>let off the hook</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Without works, without <b>fruit</b> - fruit is <b>part and parcel of the transformation</b> of a human person</li><li class=\"d0\">Demons have no works - \"everything they do is <b>evil</b>\" → doesn't help them</li></ul>"},{"t":1519.13,"lv":2,"h":"Synergy and the sheep and goats","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul over and over: <b>synergy</b> (Philippians, Romans)</li><li class=\"d1\">God working in world = <b>divine energies</b> - pouring out love, bringing justice, peace</li><li class=\"d1\">we act <b>in tandem</b> - \"cooperate in the original sense\", <b>participate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">become the <b>means</b> through which God does it</li><li class=\"d0\">God loves this person in front of me → if I'm the <b>vehicle</b> of his love → helps them <b>and transforms me</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why James's example is so pertinent - James and <b>Matthew's gospel</b> dovetail</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sheep and goats</b>: sheep to heaven, goats to hell - not for a list of doctrines, but <b>what they did</b></li><li class=\"d1\">giving food/clothing/shelter ≠ <b>earning credits</b>, hit a number, get saved</li><li class=\"d1\">Luther <b>right to reject</b> that idea</li><li class=\"d0\">Rather: you're the vehicle through which God <b>loves, clothes, heals</b> - God works through <b>means</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he doesn't have to; didn't need to create us - did it <b>out of love</b></li><li class=\"d1\">works through our actions and <b>prayers</b>, prayers of the <b>saints</b> → we participate, transformed, share his life <b>eternally</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1672.05,"lv":3,"h":"Demons as exhibit A","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Demons = <b>exhibit A</b> of never participating in what God is doing</li><li class=\"d1\">resisting, fighting, rebelling \"as hard as you can\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Person who says \"I know all that\"</li><li class=\"d1\">hears/memorized the <b>Torah</b>, knows doctrine backwards and forwards, recites the <b>Nicene Creed</b>, all the <b>canons of the ecumenical councils</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>no transformation</b> - not cooperating → not being transformed</li><li class=\"d0\">End game of salvation for Paul: <b>becoming sons of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">said over and over in <b>Romans</b>; \"like the angelic beings\"</li><li class=\"d0\">James has <b>same view</b> of salvation and faithfulness as Paul</li><li class=\"d1\">argument <b>falls apart</b> otherwise</li><li class=\"d1\">if plugging in a different definition doesn't work → maybe <b>I'm defining terms wrong</b>, not that James uses words \"totally differently than everybody else\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1790.02,"lv":2,"h":"James 2:20-23 - Abraham","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:20</b> (\"channeling Mr. T\"): without works is dead</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:21-23</b>: Abraham justified by works offering <b>Isaac</b>; faith working together with works; by works faith <b>made perfect</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness\"; <b>friend of God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Protestant and Roman Catholic treatments: <b>when was Abraham justified?</b> - \"when did Abraham get saved?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">all about <b>getting saved</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Various tacks</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Roman Catholic</b>: Paul cites another time Abraham was justified → <b>multiple times</b>; justification <b>ongoing</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>conservative Protestant</b>: James means something totally different by <b>justification</b></li><li class=\"d1\">other Protestants: they just <b>disagree</b> - Paul says this point, James that point</li><li class=\"d0\">Broader problem = concept of <b>salvation</b>, and <b>merit</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1903.38,"lv":2,"h":"Merit: Roman Catholic view","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox say Protestants and Roman Catholics have <b>more in common</b> with each other - here \"not just rhetorical\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Classical Roman Catholic</b> (\"it's all over the place now\")</li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>bar</b> of salvation: heaven not hell, eternal life not punishment</li><li class=\"d1\">can go <b>over</b> it: saints have <b>extra merit</b> → <b>treasury</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Pope can <b>dispense</b> it; saints can \"cash it in for miracles\" - \"trying not to be overly crude\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Fill up to the bar by <b>faith and works</b> - both contribute <b>merit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">medieval: <b>condign</b> and <b>congruent</b> merit - \"really complicated\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2064.49,"lv":3,"h":"Merit: classical Protestant view","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Protestant</b> = originally churches from the Reformation; now just \"not Catholic or Orthodox\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Classically did <b>not reject the paradigm</b>, but:</li><li class=\"d1\">sinner can't have <b>any merit</b> - \"all our works are <b>filthy rags</b>\" (Isaiah out of context)</li><li class=\"d1\">Calvin: <b>total depravity</b>; Luther: <b>snow-covered dunghill</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Christ's active obedience</b> accrued <b>infinite merit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">deposited in your account when you <b>believe</b> - \"total balance transfer\"</li><li class=\"d1\">your sin/demerit transferred to him, <b>punished on the cross</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Common to both: <b>saved by merit</b>, from <b>keeping the law/Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">already saw big problem in Paul</li></ul>"},{"t":2195.66,"lv":2,"h":"Merit doesn't fit transformation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not how Paul thinks Torah works - never worked that way</li><li class=\"d1\">Torah = <b>sin management system</b> until Christ came to deal with <b>sin, death, the devil</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so God could <b>dwell among sinful people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no merit for not eating pork, not murdering, <b>tassels</b>, Day of Atonement - no \"<b>gold star sticker</b>\" → heaven</li><li class=\"d0\">If salvation = <b>transformation</b>, merit concept fails</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>justification</b> = set right, back in order; <b>sanctification</b> = sharing God's holiness; <b>glorification</b> = he shares his glory</li><li class=\"d1\">\"can't bring <b>half the price of a TV</b> to Walmart and get half a TV\" - banking thing falls apart</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Drives me up a tree\" when Orthodox say <b>saved by faith and works</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>faithfulness</b> = accepting truth of things <b>and</b> living a certain way</li></ul>"},{"t":2275.02,"lv":3,"h":"Faithfulness as ongoing submission","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">How we think is <b>active</b> - not one-time \"I accept this is true\" / \"now I doubt\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ongoing submission</b> of thoughts and way of viewing world to God</li><li class=\"d1\">daily: \"this person's my <b>enemy</b>\", rude to me, I want <b>revenge</b>, <b>bitter</b></li><li class=\"d1\">continually submit our <b>understanding</b> to how God says things are</li><li class=\"d0\">Belief ≠ \"I've read and thought and <b>decided God is right</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">I'm still the <b>judge</b> of true and false</li><li class=\"d0\">Belief is the opposite</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I don't understand\"; accept I <b>can't</b>; accept I'm <b>sinful</b> and twist things to my advantage</li><li class=\"d1\">submit to what God says \"even when I really don't like it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Ongoing: <b>transformed by renewing of our mind</b> (<i>nous</i>) - how we see reality</li><li class=\"d0\">Also <b>how we live</b> - even <b>physically</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2448.74,"lv":2,"h":"Abraham: a journey of transformation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hard life, wrong path → shows in <b>physical appearance</b>; repentance → life transforms, <b>shows</b> too (\"total transformation\")</li><li class=\"d1\">salvation encompasses all - how <b>Paul and James</b> both speak</li><li class=\"d0\">With that view, Abraham <b>not a problem</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no need to find the moment he stopped heading to <b>hell</b> and started heading to <b>heaven</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Many moments in Abraham's life</li><li class=\"d1\">command: <b>\"Walk before me and be righteous\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">starts in <b>Ur</b>, typical <b>Sumerian</b> → by end \"someone very different\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why we read <b>saints' lives</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Mary of Egypt</b>: starts in one place → \"beacon of holiness\" through <b>repentance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul cites one moment in the journey, James another → <b>don't contradict</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2547.27,"lv":3,"h":"The change is in us, not God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Transformation isn't happening in <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"God was angry, now happy with me\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>especially not</b> \"when he looks at me he sees <b>Jesus</b>, not me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"he'd still hate me, but loves Jesus - I sneak in like a <b>Trojan horse</b> past his wrath\" - \"Nah\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God doesn't need to change - <b>perfect, holy</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>We</b> need to change; change is happening <b>in Abraham</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2597.24,"lv":3,"h":"\"Justified\" - set in order","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:21</b> again: \"Was Abraham our father justified?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Justification in <b>word processing</b> = setting things in order</li><li class=\"d1\">right-justified, left-justified, <b>full</b> justified - lined up</li><li class=\"d0\">So: was Abraham <b>set right, transformed</b>, made more like God by <b>works</b> offering Isaac?</li><li class=\"d1\">not enough to sit at camp: \"Oh yeah, God, I'd be <b>willing</b> to sacrifice my son\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"No\" - he <b>went</b> and was willing to go through with it</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Faith working together with his works\" - <b>faithfulness</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Perfect</b> = <b>complete</b>, not \"no sins\"</li><li class=\"d1\">faithfulness made complete by <b>actually going and doing it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Abraham believed God\" = same Greek word → <b>was faithful</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2723.16,"lv":2,"h":"Accounted righteousness; no single moment","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Accounted</b> - not an <b>accountant</b> (\"Jules isn't here\") adding <b>merit and demerit</b>, balance sheet</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>share he received was righteousness</b> - transformed, justified, because <b>faithful</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not looking for one moment of justification</li><li class=\"d0\">James could as well say: justified by works when he <b>left Ur</b> for unknown land</li><li class=\"d1\">Yahweh said \"Go... I'm going to give you this land\"</li><li class=\"d1\">right response not \"<b>I believe you</b>\" - it was to <b>get up and go</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not sit in Ur and <b>assent</b> that descendants will get the land</li><li class=\"d0\">Any number of examples from Abraham's life</li><li class=\"d0\">Not an argument between James and Paul over <b>when</b> - \"there is <b>no moment of justification</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2781.38,"lv":2,"h":"James 2:24 - \"faith alone\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:24</b>: \"a man is justified by works, and not by <b>faith alone</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>NKJV</b> \"trying to slip this past you\" - says \"by faith only\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Only place \"<b>by faith alone</b>\" occurs in NT</li><li class=\"d1\">Luther added <b>alone</b> to <b>Romans 3</b> - and there it has no \"<b>not by</b>\" in front</li><li class=\"d0\">Doesn't mean, as <b>Roman Catholic</b> friends assert, saved by <b>combo</b> of faith and works - \"piled up\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Means justified, set right, by <b>doing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just by <b>faithfulness</b> in the sense of claiming loyalty to God</li></ul>"},{"t":2839.82,"lv":2,"h":"James 2:25 - Rahab","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:25</b>: \"was not <b>Rahab the harlot</b> also justified by works\" - received messengers, sent them another way</li><li class=\"d1\">less talked about; \"the <b>parallel example</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Rahab = <b>prostitute</b> by trade, not just pejorative</li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"you did this nice thing for some Jews, therefore saved\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Go back to the OT story</b> - problem: we treat it as \"weird proof text\"</li><li class=\"d1\">she'd heard of <b>Egypt, plagues</b>, the wilderness → <b>fears</b> the God of Israel</li><li class=\"d1\">decided to <b>switch sides</b>: \"you are going to win, your God is greater than the gods that rule around here\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Loyalties/faithfulness</b> switched → expressed in <b>sheltering the spies</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"justified\" as in going to heaven for one nice thing - it <b>transformed her life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">becomes an <b>Israelite</b>, joins them</li><li class=\"d1\">in <b>genealogy of Christ</b> read at Liturgy a couple weeks ago</li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>Mary of Egypt</b> - that's what James means by salvation and justification: <b>transformation of life</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3011.5,"lv":2,"h":"James 2:26 - body without spirit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:26</b>: \"as the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is dead also\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Faithfulness never expressed in works = <b>body without a soul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"just an <b>object</b>... a thing\" - no life</li><li class=\"d0\">What is <b>life</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>growth and transformation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">inert, not growing or changing at all → <b>not alive, by definition</b></li></ul>"}]});
